.Dd November 11, 2026
.Dt PKEY_ALLOC 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pkey_alloc ,
.Nm pkey_free ,
.Nm pkey_mprotect
.Nd allocate, free and assign memory protection keys
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/mman.h
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_alloc "unsigned int flags" "unsigned int access_rights"
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_free "int pkey"
.Ft int
.Fn pkey_mprotect "void *addr" "size_t len" "int prot" "int pkey"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These calls manage the protection keys of an address space, which
.Xr pkeys 7
describes: the x86-64 kernel turns the keys on where the processor has
them, and a program sees the OSPKE bit of CPUID leaf 7 set.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_alloc
allocates the lowest free key from 0 to 15 and writes
.Fa access_rights
for it into the calling thread's PKRU:
.Dv PKEY_DISABLE_ACCESS
(1) denies every data access to the pages of the key, and
.Dv PKEY_DISABLE_WRITE
(2) denies writes to them.
Other threads keep their own PKRU.
.Fa flags
must be 0.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_free
frees a key that
.Fn pkey_alloc
gave, or key 0, which every address space starts with allocated;
once freed, key 0 is the first that
.Fn pkey_alloc
gives again, as on Linux.
Pages that still carry the key keep it; a later
.Fn pkey_alloc
may give it again, and its new rights then apply to them.
.Pp
.Fn pkey_mprotect
is
.Xr mprotect 2
that also gives the pages of the range the key
.Fa pkey ,
which must be allocated.
With
.Fa pkey
-1 it is
.Fn mprotect
itself: the pages keep their keys.
The key is kept through a split or merge of the mapping, by
.Xr mremap 2 ,
and by the child of
.Xr fork 2 ,
which inherits the allocated keys and the parent's PKRU.
.Xr execve 2
frees every key but 0 and starts the program with PKRU 0x55555554.
.Pp
.Xr mprotect 2
or
.Xr mmap 2
with
.Dv PROT_EXEC
alone gives the pages the execute-only key, which the kernel allocates once
for the address space and denies all data access in PKRU, so that the
pages run but cannot be read.
.Fn pkey_alloc
passes it over, and
.Fn pkey_free
and
.Fn pkey_mprotect
refuse it.
A later
.Fn mprotect
with any other protection moves such pages back to key 0.
.Pp
Without the processor's keys, on x86-64 and as Linux answers there,
.Fn pkey_alloc
fails with
.Er ENOSPC ,
.Fn pkey_free
with
.Er EINVAL
for every key, and
.Fn pkey_mprotect
is
.Fn mprotect
with key -1 and fails with
.Er EINVAL
for any other key once the range is checked.
Linux builds the three calls on x86-64 alone: the i686 kernel has no
entry for them and answers
.Er ENOSYS .
The x86-64 kernel answers an i386 program as it answers a 64-bit one,
with the keys on where they are on.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn pkey_alloc
returns the key.
.Fn pkey_free
and
.Fn pkey_mprotect
return 0.
On an error each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set; the kernel returns the negative error number.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width "[ENOSPC]"
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn pkey_alloc
was given a flag, or a right other than the two.
.Fn pkey_free
or
.Fn pkey_mprotect
was given a key that is not allocated, the execute-only key, or a key
outside 0 to 15 other than -1 for
.Fn pkey_mprotect .
.Fn pkey_mprotect
was given an address that is not page-aligned, or a protection
.Fn mprotect
refuses.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
.Fn pkey_mprotect
was given a range that wraps or that is not all mapped.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
.Fn pkey_alloc
has no free key.
.It Bq Er ENOSYS
The kernel is the i686 one.
.El
.Pp
.Fn pkey_mprotect
checks in Linux's order: the address's alignment, an empty range, which
returns 0 at once, a range that wraps, the protection and the key, then the
mapping.
It fails otherwise as
.Xr mprotect 2
does.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr mprotect 2 ,
.Xr mseal 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr pkeys 7
